{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And he said, What is this you have done to us, etc.</b> It is written concerning Avimelech, ״עָשִׂיתָ לָּנוּ״ — in the plural, and so Avimelech said to Abraham (Genesis 20:9), ״מַה עָשִׂיתָ לָּנוּ״, etc., whereas Pharaoh said to Abraham, ״מַה זֹּאת עָשִׂיתָ לִּי״ (Genesis 12:18) — in the singular. And in the book Toldot Yitzhak he wrote to resolve this difficulty, that since the Egyptians are steeped in lust, there it makes no difference whether he said 'she is my sister' or 'she is my wife' — in any case they would have committed immorality with her. 'But to me alone did you sin,' for I am a king — shall the Judge of all the earth not do justice? Therefore you should have told me the truth. But Avimelech said 'to us' — 'to all of us you have sinned,' for I and my people, all of us are righteous, and they would not have touched her at all if you had told the truth.'" "And this answer is not sufficient, for behold we see that the Egyptians, even though they are steeped in lust, nevertheless did not touch her; rather they said, 'This one is fitting for the king,' as it says, ״וַיְהַלְלוּ אוֹתָהּ אֶל פַּרְעֹה״. And it appears to me that since the Egyptians are the brothers of the Cushites and they are black, they are not accustomed to a beautiful woman, as Rashi explained in parashat Lekh Lekha (Genesis 12:11) — presumably they do not lay hand upon a beautiful woman. And we have seen that this is indeed so, for when they saw Sarah they said among themselves, ״הֲגוּנָה זוֹ לַמֶּלֶךְ״ — if so, they did not wish to take what was not fitting for them. And if so, Pharaoh spoke well when he said ״מַה זֹּאת עָשִׂיתָ לִּי״, but not 'to my people,' for in any case they would not have touched her, for out of fear of the monarchy they would surely not touch what was fitting for the king alone and not for them. But the people of Avimelech were not black and were accustomed to beautiful women, and there is no fear of the monarchy upon them in this matter, and everyone lays hand upon a beautiful unattached woman — if so, 'to us, to all of us you have sinned.' And a proof for this: behold, concerning Avimelech it is not said, ״וַיְהַלְלוּ אוֹתָהּ אֶל אֲבִימֶלֶךְ״ to say 'this one is fitting for him' — in a matter such as this the people are like the king; therefore he said ״מַה זֹּאת עָשִׂיתָ לָּנוּ״. And as for what Pharaoh said, ״הִנֵּה אִשְׁתְּךָ קַח וָלֵךְ״, it is because he feared that after the king released her from his house they would say that the king did not desire her, and they would lay hand upon her — which is not so with Avimelech, for it was specifically the Egyptians who were steeped in lust."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:30:24.517070532Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}